Exercise: sa-review-final (chapter 22 capstone)

Deliver a read-only status page on servera for the partner serverb. Work with
sudo or as root. Do not stop firewalld and do not leave SELinux permissive.

Identity and software
 1. Group ops (GID 4900); account statusadm (UID 3100, primary group ops,
    shell /sbin/nologin); account auditor (UID 3101).
 2. httpd installed, running and enabled.

Content
 3. /srv/status owned by statusadm:ops, mode 2750. In it index.html with the
    line: Status page: ready    (owner statusadm, group ops, mode 640)
 4. The web user (apache) and auditor may read the page and the directory
    through ACL entries; new files in the directory inherit them.

Service and labels
 5. httpd serves /srv/status on port 8095 (a file in /etc/httpd/conf.d/).
 6. The content type httpd_sys_content_t is stored as a mapping for
    /srv/status and everything below, and applied. Port 8095 has the label
    http_port_t. SELinux stays enforcing.

Network
 7. A zone named statuspartners with serverb's address as its source and
    port 8095/tcp. The default zone does not open that port.

Logs and recovery
 8. The journal is persistent (drop-in 10-persistent.conf in
    /etc/systemd/journald.conf.d/).
 9. status-backup.service (one-shot) saves /srv/status into
    /srv/backups/status.tar.gz with tar, keeping ACLs and SELinux labels.
    status-backup.timer runs it daily and is enabled. Run the service once.

Prove it
10. Check from serverb: curl http://servera:8095/ . Reboot servera, check again,
    and grade again.

On workstation
11. Fill in answers.txt.

Then, on workstation:  lab grade sa-review-final
